# Target C-II — aggregate detour forces a stronger route-currency floor

**Date:** 2026-09-13
**Status:** proof-only composition; theorem-state authority unchanged.
**LEVEL I STATUS:** **STILL OPEN. C-II REMAINS OPEN.**

This note keeps the Section-122 aggregate detour demand before applying the coarse
bound `f<=Q`. On the Section-118 endpoint/common-cap profile, the exact Section-121
face identity and the endpoint harmonic diameter-moment floor imply

\[
\boxed{
\liminf_{D\to\infty}
\left({b-\operatorname{per}(F)\over D}+{Q+4f\over D}\right)
\ge 2S_*+c_0-\pi
=2.8764935780\ldots .
}
\tag{AR.1}
\]

Here

\[
S_*=2.798596312209462\ldots,
\qquad
c_0={\pi\over2}(2-\sqrt3).
\]

The bound is a route-currency statement. It does not provide an upper bound on the
same currency and does not close C-II.

## 1. Exact finite-to-asymptotic reduction

Use the post-ZF Type-II notation of Sections 121--123. Let

\[
E_\Sigma=\sum_r(L_r+P_{{\rm exc},r}-2d_r),
\qquad
P_{\rm exc}=Q+4f,
\qquad
t_D={q-k\over D},
\qquad
s_D={d_\Sigma\over D}.
\]

The exact aggregate face-incidence identity is

\[
E_\Sigma
 =4\Delta_R+4f+2(q-k-e_{BB}+C_{\rm att})-2d_\Sigma,
\tag{AR.2}
\]

where `Delta_R=R_4-sum_r R_r` and `C_att` counts loaded-loaded contacts reused by
the harmonic cells. In Type II, `c_B=0`, so every loaded-loaded contact is a force-
hull side. Pairwise interior-disjoint attachment cells use a force-hull side at most
once, hence

\[
C_{\rm att}\le e_{BB}.
\]

The audited full-core localization gives `Delta_R=o(D)`. Therefore (AR.2) gives the
one-sided finite estimate

\[
E_\Sigma
\le 4\Delta_R+4f+2(q-k)-2d_\Sigma.
\tag{AR.3}
\]

The canonical Section-122 detour theorem gives

\[
\liminf {E_\Sigma\over D}\ge c_0.
\tag{AR.4}
\]

On the Section-118 endpoint/common-cap profile, the audited complement-gap theorem
gives

\[
\liminf s_D\ge S_*.
\tag{AR.5}
\]

Since `b+Q=2(q-k)` exactly and `per(F)/D->pi`, define

\[
\delta_D={b-\operatorname{per}(F)\over D}.
\]

Then the exact outer ledger is

\[
{Q\over D}=2t_D-{\operatorname{per}(F)\over D}-\delta_D.
\tag{AR.6}
\]

Substitute `4f=P_exc-Q` into (AR.3), then use (AR.6):

\[
{E_\Sigma\over D}
\le {P_{\rm exc}\over D}
 +{\operatorname{per}(F)\over D}+\delta_D-2s_D+o(1).
\tag{AR.7}
\]

Combining (AR.4), (AR.5), and `per(F)/D->pi` yields

\[
\liminf\left({P_{\rm exc}\over D}+\delta_D\right)
\ge 2S_*+c_0-\pi.
\tag{AR.8}
\]

This is (AR.1).

## 2. Safe numerical certificate

The displayed decimal is not used as an exact input. The rational enclosures

\[
S_*>2.7985,
\qquad
3.14159<\pi<3.14160,
\qquad
\sqrt3<1.73206
\]

give the outward-safe margin

\[
2S_*+c_0-\pi>2.8762.
\tag{AR.9}
\]

The accompanying verifier checks (AR.9) and a symbolic rational instance of the
finite cancellation. The exact value in (AR.1) is only the evaluation of the
symbolic constant using the audited endpoint value of `S_*`.

## 3. What this changes

The earlier train-boundary floor only used

\[
\delta_D+P_{\rm exc}/D
\ge4\sqrt{\pi/(2\sqrt3)}-\pi
=0.6676585738\ldots .
\]

The aggregate detour theorem instead pays for the harmonic-cell diameter moment and
raises the endpoint/common-cap route currency to `2.8762+o(1)`. Combining (AR.8) with
`f<=Q` recovers the existing endpoint scalar floor

\[
{q-k\over D}
\ge {4\pi+2S_*+c_0\over10}-o(1)
=1.8584456846\ldots-o(1),
\]

and the audited orbit-capacity composition still excludes the endpoint-profile range
`(q-k)/D<=1.8585`. The stronger route floor does not control the high-detour region:
there is still no upper bound on `delta_D+P_exc/D`, no source-preserving strict-
diameter competitor, and no forced unit equilateral triangle.

**Classification:** proved candidate awaiting independent audit; proof-only and scoped
to the Section-118 endpoint/common-cap profile. The theorem state remains
`2026-09-12.audit.46`; C-II remains open.
